macOS 27.3 adds native Markdown rendering to Quick Look, allowing users to preview .md files with their formatting applied.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/11/quick-look-markdown-macos.jpg" alt="" width="1920" height="1080" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1075608" /><br/>
In the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/11/03/apple-releases-first-macos-golden-gate-27-3-beta/">first beta</a> of macOS 27.3, Quick Look displays styled headings, bold text, lists, and links when previewing Markdown documents. Selecting a file in Finder and pressing the Space bar brings up a readable preview, without requiring a separate editor or Quick Look extension.<br/>
<br/>
The addition builds on Apple's broader adoption of Markdown. Notes gained <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/how-to/ios-import-export-markdown-apple-notes/">Markdown import and export</a> with <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ios-26/">iOS 26</a>, while <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ios-27/">iOS 27</a> introduced <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/06/17/ios-27-apple-notes-app-new-features/">Markdown copy and paste</a>, allowing users to paste Markdown as formatted text or copy rich text in Markdown format.<br/>
<br/>
Apple's new <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/siri/">Siri</a> AI app skills also use Markdown. According to Apple's <a href="https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/orchestrating-complex-actions-with-app-skills">developer documentation</a>, developers can bundle SKILL.md files with their apps to explain how &zwnj;Siri&zwnj; should handle tasks involving app actions. <br/>
<br/>
